{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no gaps.\n\n**Obligation.** When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration MUST be in the mandatory input set defined by `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`. That requirement is cited as context, so I did not reassess its own implementation or tests.\n\n**Code.**\n- `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` carries the `{impl}` for this requirement.\n- It looks up the subject with `definitions.get(subject_name)`, filtering out only `DefinitionKind::Term`. A named example is therefore accepted as a subject.\n- It always seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`, so the subject's own declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- That selector goes through `source_input`, then `InputSelector::resolve` in `src/inputs.rs`. `resolve` skips Terms and includes every other matching definition, examples included. It emits `crate::redaction::redact_lines(&definition.raw_lines)`, which is the full raw declaration with its `:::{...}` fences.\n- The model input from `to_model_input` carries `requirements`, `matches` and `text`. The mandatory inputs shown above for the requirement subject already include the `:::{req}` and closing `:::` lines, which shows the fences are kept.\n- `generated_evidence_selector` never omits a `Requirements` selector, so the declaration cannot be dropped as generated evidence.\n- Deduplication through `insert_selector` does not remove it.\n\n**Test.**\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` carries the `{test}` reference to `requirements.examples.authoring_inputs`.\n- It defines `:::{example} case`, calls `AssessmentSources::prepare(root, \"requirements.case\")`, and asserts `subject_kind` is `Definition(DefinitionKind::Example, false)`.\n- It also asserts `example.mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the example's own declaration is the first mandatory input.\n- The test does not assert the fence lines in the text. The text comes from the same `resolve` code that produces the requirement declarations, so I judged this enough.\n\n**Marking and unspecified behavior.** The `{impl}` and `{test}` references are in place on the code and the test. I found no unmarked implementing code and no unspecified extra behavior for this requirement.",
  "references": "b899630e41f440f0f3cdb55b167fbf32959bdbf515b06e0008b680021fa1d4d4",
